  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another frosty morning Tuesday, with lows again\nbelow freezing especially just inland from the coast. Closer to\nthe water, temps near sunrise will be closer to the mid 30s,\nwith the current frost advisory looking good for Mainland Hyde,\nPamlico and eastern Carteret Counties. Wouldn't be surprised to\nsee upper 20s in our usual cold spots.  \n\nLow-level thicknesses will steadily warm mid to late week, and even \nthough high pressure will remain in place, temps will warm to\nabove climo with 70s inland. For the OBX, highs will be \ntempered by light northeast breezes off the cooler waters of \nthe Labrador Current as water temps have cooled to the upper\n50s. Overnight mins again Tuesday night will approach freezing\ninland, before a gradual trend upward by weeks end. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure over the Ohio Valley will dominate\nour weather all week, settling overhead by the end of the week,\nand off the coast by Saturday. As a result dry conditions will\npersist with plenty of sunshine. Fire weather concerns may grow\nwith the curing vegetation combined with low RH values each \nafternoon. Luckily winds will not be an issue with a light \ngradient in place. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...There is increasing potential for a coastal low\nto gain strength off the FL coast late in the week. While there\nis much uncertainty on where the system tracks, the last \nseveral model cycles of both the deterministic and AI models \nhave indicated a northeastward track off the Southeast US coast.\nAI guidance continues to show a track closer to eastern NC and \nthe latest 0Z GFS is trending in this direction. We will \ncontinue to monitor the system, as large waves and associated \ncoastal impacts are becoming more likely along the Outer Banks.",
